[about]
Title=1 - User Interface
Intro=What _APP_ is made of and the rules its keys follow: its window, its main view, a dialog, the Say keys, the menus, then the key rules and the four places help lives. Each part is named by the narrator and then shown by the screen reader.
Setup=_APP_ is installed and your reader is running.
Homework=Open _APP_ and guess three keys from their words, then check each with Control plus F1.

; SHOW, THEN TELL. Every step names one part in a sentence or two, presses the
; key that brings it to focus, and gives the reader's words, word for word:
; name, role, value, state, then the hint after a pause. checkTutorial holds
; this walk to it: never more than two host-only steps in a row, and the reader
; in at least half the steps. The placeholders below are in that shape; replace
; each with what _APP_ really says, confirmed with buildTutorials -live.

[step]
Say=_APP_ is heard before it is seen. I name each part; the reader shows it: name, kind, value and state, then a hint.
Key=
Hear=

[step]
Say=The window. Its desktop key opens it or brings it forward. Alt plus Control plus the program's letter.
Key=Alt+Control+A
Hear=CHANGE ME: the window's title, word for word as the reader says it
Hear=CHANGE ME: the program's greeting, word for word
Note=Use _APP_'s own desktop key.

[step]
Say=The main view: name, role, value, then the hint.
Key=Tab
Hear=CHANGE ME: the main view's name, its role, its value, word for word as the reader says it
Hear=CHANGE ME: its hint, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=A dialog: a label, then the control it names. Tab moves in the order the controls were added; OK and Cancel come last.
Key=Control+K
Hear=CHANGE ME: the dialog's title, word for word as the reader says it
Hear=CHANGE ME: the first label and its control, word for word as the reader says it
Note=Use a dialog _APP_ really has, opened by its own key.

[step]
Say=Enter presses OK, Escape is Cancel, Control plus Enter submits from anywhere, and Alt plus an underlined letter jumps to its control.
Key=Escape
Hear=CHANGE ME: the main window's title again, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=The Say keys ask and change nothing. Shift plus Z is Say Status.
Key=Shift+Z
Hear=CHANGE ME: the status line, in one breath, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=Menus say their own keys. F10 opens the menu bar, and Down Arrow reaches the first item.
Key=F10
Hear=Menu bar
Hear=CHANGE ME: the first menu's name, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=The item, its hotkey, then its letter.
Key=Down Arrow
Hear=CHANGE ME: the first item, its hotkey, its letter, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=Now the rules behind every key. The word gives the letter; Control does; Shift and Alt ask; adding Shift reverses; Alt plus Shift is a command with no control of its own.
Key=
Hear=

[step]
Say=The function keys follow Windows. Anything with Insert is the screen reader's own: Insert plus T says the title.
Key=Insert+T
Hear=CHANGE ME: the window's title, word for word as the reader says it

[step]
Say=Control plus F1 is the Key Describer: each key says what it does instead of doing it.
Key=Control+F1
Hear=Key Describer On

[step]
Say=Help is in four places. I say which; the reader says the key. The guide.
Key=
Hear=F1, the guide

[step]
Say=The changes, release by release; the version; the name of any key.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus F1, History
Hear=Alt plus F1, About
Hear=Control plus F1, Key Describer

[step]
Say=What this walk taught. I name the part; the reader says the key.
Key=
Hear=

[step]
Say=Say Status.
Key=
Hear=Shift plus Z
